## What this does not solve

**A brief has no reader, and that is its largest defect.** Every reading in section 8 is taken,
compared and reported by the same agent that made the change. `OPERATING-THE-PLAN.md` §4 —
*a repair is work, and work does not certify itself* — applies here and there is no adversary in
a brief to satisfy it. The only mitigation this shape offers is that section 8 demands the
readings **verbatim** in the report, so a human can be the second pair of eyes. That is weaker
than an independent reader and should not be mistaken for one. If the work is important enough
that self-certification is not acceptable, it is important enough for a plan directory.

**Nothing here is enforced.** No gate reads a brief. `validate-plan.py` will never see it, there
is no contract hash, and nothing detects a brief that quietly dropped section 8.

**The evidence is one afternoon.** Six briefs, one operator, one VPS, one job shape — a static
site onto a server with neighbours. The six were `BRIEF.md`, `FOLLOWUP.md`, `TLS.md`, `GRAPH.md`,
`TRIM.md` and `CHART-DIAG.md`, 665 lines in total. The operator had both Grillin and Smokin
installed, produced no plan directory, and never ran Smokin once — the fifth consecutive job
where the ideas transferred and the tools did not. Whether this shape holds for jobs that are not
deployments is untested. What is measured is narrower and worth stating exactly: the same nine
sections were re-derived by hand six times in one day.
